{%- comment -%}
  "Notify me when it's back" — the out-of-stock path.

  Usage: {% render 'back-in-stock', product: product, variant: variant %}

  Before this, a sold-out size gave the shopper a greyed-out button and
  nothing else: the page was a dead end, and the demand it represented went
  unrecorded. Back-in-stock is also the highest-earning automated flow there
  is — ahead of abandoned cart — but only for stores that capture the
  address in the first place, which is this.

  ── Why a contact form ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A theme cannot write to Shopify. There is no storefront API for "remember
  this person wants this variant", so the alternatives are an app or an
  email. This uses Shopify's built-in contact form, which posts and arrives
  as a normal contact-form notification carrying the product, the variant
  and its id — enough for the merchant to send the alert by hand, or to
  forward into Klaviyo as a trigger.

  It is deliberately the smallest thing that works end to end today. When an
  app is added, replace the inner fields and keep the surrounding markup:
  app.js only looks for [data-notify-block], [data-notify-variant] and
  [data-notify-variant-label].

  ── The success state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Two things make this less obvious than it looks.

  First, form.posted_successfully? only exists INSIDE the form block — read
  outside it the variable is nil, which is falsy, so a success check wrapped
  around the whole snippet would silently never fire. The branch therefore
  sits inside the form.

  Second, Shopify handles a successful post by redirecting back to this URL
  with ?contact_posted=true. That is a fresh page load, so the selected
  variant is whatever the URL says — usually an available one — and this
  block would render hidden with the confirmation sealed inside it. app.js
  reads that parameter and reveals the block, which is why the markup below
  carries data-notify-posted for it to find.

  ── Marketing consent ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The opt-in checkbox is unticked, and it is separate from the alert itself.
  Asking to be told about one bottle is not agreeing to a newsletter, and
  bundling the two is the "basket sneaking" pattern the CCPA guidelines name
  — the same rule that governs auto-added gifts. The alert is sent either
  way; only the marketing list depends on the tick.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign bis_variant = variant | default: product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign is_oos = true
  if bis_variant.available
    assign is_oos = false
  endif
  assign variant_label = ''
  if bis_variant.title != 'Default Title'
    assign variant_label = bis_variant.title
  endif
-%}

<div class="bis-block" data-notify-block{% unless is_oos %} hidden{% endunless %}>
  {%- form 'contact', class: 'bis-form', id: 'back-in-stock-form' -%}
    {%- if form.posted_successfully? -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        data-notify-posted is the flag app.js looks for when it sees
        ?contact_posted=true in the URL — see the header note.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="bis-done" role="status" data-notify-posted>
        <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">mark_email_read</span>
        <div>
          <strong>You're on the list.</strong>
          <p>We'll email you as soon as this one is back. No other mail — just this bottle.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- else -%}
      <div class="bis-head">
        <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">notifications_active</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Sold out{% if variant_label != blank %} in <span data-notify-variant-label>{{ variant_label }}</span>{% endif %}</strong>
          <p>Leave your email and we'll tell you the moment it's back in stock.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        The merchant reads this in a plain email, so the body has to carry
        everything needed to act on it — including the variant id, which is
        the only unambiguous handle on "which size".
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="contact[subject]" value="Back in stock request">
      <input type="hidden" name="contact[product]" value="{{ product.title | escape }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="contact[product_url]" value="{{ shop.url }}{{ product.url }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="contact[variant_id]" value="{{ bis_variant.id }}" data-notify-variant>
      <input type="hidden" name="contact[body]"
             value="Notify me when {{ product.title | escape }}{% if variant_label != blank %} ({{ variant_label | escape }}){% endif %} is back in stock.">

      <div class="bis-row">
        {%- comment -%}
          A real <label>, not a placeholder. Placeholders vanish on focus and
          are not announced as labels — WCAG, and also the reason mistyped
          emails go unnoticed. Visually hidden because the heading above
          already says what the field is for.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <label class="visually-hidden" for="bis-email">Email address</label>
        <input class="bis-input"
               id="bis-email"
               type="email"
               name="contact[email]"
               autocomplete="email"
               inputmode="email"
               required
               placeholder="you@example.com"
               aria-describedby="bis-privacy">
        <button type="submit" class="btn-g bis-submit">
          <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">notifications</span>Notify me
        </button>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        A div, not a p: default_errors renders a <ul>, and a list inside a
        paragraph is invalid HTML that browsers silently restructure.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if form.errors -%}
        <div class="bis-error" role="alert">{{ form.errors | default_errors }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}

      <label class="bis-optin">
        <input type="checkbox" name="contact[marketing_opt_in]" value="yes">
        <span>Also send me new arrivals and offers. Optional — the stock alert comes either way.</span>
      </label>

      <p class="bis-privacy" id="bis-privacy">
        We use your address for this alert only. See our
        <a href="{{ shop.privacy_policy.url | default: '/pages/privacy-policy' }}">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endform -%}
</div>
